# the risc-v vector rules: the v extension's loads, stores, lanewise
# operations, splat, extract, insert and reductions, the guards they read and
# the immediates they compute
#
# a vector the v unit holds is in one register at lmul 1, its lanes from lane
# 0 at the low bits, and the bits above it are undefined, so a rule reads no
# lane past the vector's and may write any (rvv 1.0, section 4.5 "mapping for
# lmul = 1"). its length is the build's vlen, which only a zvl extension
# bounds, so every rule sets the vector length it runs at itself: it begins
# with vsetivli x0, n, vtype, or with addi and vsetvli x0, t, vtype where n
# does not fit vsetivli's five bits (section 6 "configuration-setting
# instructions"), n the vector's lane count, and every vector instruction
# after it states that vtype, so the configure step finds it in force and
# adds nothing. the vtype is tail and mask agnostic and lmul 1, but an insert
# keeps the tail undisturbed and a narrowing runs at a fractional lmul. no
# rule is masked.
#
# a load or a store is vle or vse at the lane width where the access is
# aligned to a lane, and vle8 or vse8 of the vector's bytes otherwise, since a
# misaligned element may trap (section 7.3). a lane's index is the root's
# immediate, which the computed sources turn into the offset of vslidedown and
# vslideup and the vector length an insert writes up to. an extract slides
# the lane down to lane 0 and moves it out by vmv.x.s or vfmv.f.s, and an
# insert moves the scalar into lane 0 of a temporary and slides it up into
# the vector under a vector length of the lane plus one, tail undisturbed
# (sections 16.1 to 16.3).
#
# a reduction reads its seed from lane 0 of a register (section 14): the
# vector itself where the operation is idempotent, a minimum, maximum, and or
# or, and a lane of zero, or of negative zero for a float sum, made by vmv.s.x
# and vfsgnjn.vv, which is mirl's choice of identity. a reduction of one lane
# is that lane. vfredosum adds in lane order from the seed, as reduce.fadd.seq
# does, and vfredusum in an order the implementation picks, which the ir
# leaves free. vfredmin, vfredmax, vfmin and vfmax give the other operand for
# one nan operand and the canonical nan for two (section 13.11), as fminnum and
# fmaxnum do on risc-v. rvv has no product reduction and no ieee minimum or
# maximum, so those stay lane by lane.

